So, Myrddin the gnomish wizard was happily traipsing through the dungeon 
on alt.org.  He had resolved early on that he would be happy with what 
he found in the dungeon, and wouldn't wish for anything given the 
chance, nor would he genocide creatures, nor change shape, nor would he 
polymorph items.
Something sinister lurked in the game, determined to test his resolve. 
In Sokoban, he found a wand of polymorph.  On another early level, a 
ring of poly control.  But he exercised his restraint.  In the toolshop 
of the gnomish mines, he found a magic lamp.  "Yes!  Silver Dragon Scale 
... no!"  Though sorely tempted, he kept to his resolve, but appreciated 
the perpetual light.
At the top of Sokoban, he found a bag of holding.  He felt himself 
fairly well prepared with that, but the lack of reflection was troubling.
He was put in grave danger several times from the elemental breath 
attacks of some of the scarier monsters in the dungeon, such as winter 
wolves.
A random spellbook on Dlvl 7 turned out to be 'identify'.  There was 
much rejoicing in the dungeon that day.
He descended with trepidation deeper into the dungeon.  He knew that a 
randomly generated wand of cold, lightning (OW!), or fire would kill him 
very quickly.  Going down one set of stairs, he found himself in a vast 
room with no cover, and about twenty monsters who looked... hungry. 
Many of them could potentially have wands.  "No way to reflect beams is 
a serious drawback... maybe just a little wish.... NO."  He looked at 
his repertoire of attack spells, hoping for a solution.  Force Bolt 
and... that's it.
Next, he checked his huge stack of throwing daggers.  Yep, all 3 of 
them, still there.  He sighed.  The lore for handling a situation like 
this was to create a boulder fort, and blast everything that came near 
with magic missiles.  This plan fell short on two counts.  No magic 
missiles, and a gelatinous cube which would happily clean up the loot on 
the floor while Myrddin was waiting in the fort.
Taking a deep breath, he charged through the gaps in the crowds, hoping 
beyond hope that none of them got in a lucky beam shot.  He scooped up 
all the relevant loot, and used his force bolt and the Magicbane 
liberally to keep the crowds at bay.  And he ran.  A lot.
Miraculously, he survived.
Later on, he found a spellbook of magic mapping.  By this point, he knew 
he had a chance to recover the Amulet.  But he still needed a few more 
things before being reasonably prepared for what lie ahead.
While pondering the problem of how to get reflection, he stumbled over a 
saddle someone had just left lying about.  A *saddle*!
Then, a plan hit him like a thunderbolt (though without his rings 
exploding).  He knew if he found Ludios, the dragons guarding the castle 
might be silver, and might be persuaded to donate their scales to the 
cause.  He ran (carefully) through the dungeon, magic mapping every 
level looking for vaults.  The second vault he found transported him to 
Ludios.  Fickle fate had deemed that he should have both intrinsic and 
extrinsic telepathy, but no blindfold.  Fortunately, he also had the 
spell 'detect monsters'.  Upon casting, he jumped for joy to realize 
that *two* of the dragons on the level were silver.
With the help of his pets, he cleared out the zoo.  There was a 
cockatrice in the zoo, whose corpse proved *very* useful.  He made 
double sure he had gloves on, picked it up, and blessed it.  Then he 
opened the door from a diagonal.  Whenever a soldier presented his face 
through the door, he whacked it with the corpse.  You'd figure after the 
first ten or so soldiers got petrified at the same spot the others would 
catch on, but no...  a whole army of statues.  He carefully snuck up on 
the silver dragon, and poked it full of Magicbane sized holes.  It 
collapsed under the hail of his protracted attack.  And left no scales. 
    He resurrected it, and upon it's second death, a shiny set of silver 
dragon scales dropped to the floor.
He quickly put on and enchanted the scales, and breathed a sign of 
relief.  Next, he resurrected the poor dragon again, having felt sorry 
for it.  So he played it some very attractive music, and it liked the 
music so much it started following him around like a puppy.  All the way 
back to the saddle he'd found, a few levels below.
By then, he felt ready to take on anything, and went to visit his old 
mentor, who told him that a villain had stolen the Eye of the 
Aethiopica.  He flew down (literally, as he was practicing riding on his 
pet dragon) and confronted the Dark One.  Who croaked.  Quite 
anticlimactically, I might add.
The next milestone was when he cleared out the castle, and found the 
wand of wishing tucked away in the corner.  He sighed to himself, 
thinking of all the things he *could* have.
He cleared out the armory, finding 2 more types of magic boots.
As he cleared out the Valley of the Dead, looking for more attack 
spells, a vampire death dropped a wand.  He looked... a wand of wishing. 
  Yousonofa... Grumbling, he put *that* wand in his bag, too.  He didn't 
find magic missile in the Valley, so he bought protection from the 
priest, and decided the next likely spot would probably be Vlad's tower.

The first high level Demon he met was Juiblex.  Jui was sooo slow, he 
made it half way across the level before the thing could muster itself 
to make an appearance.  Upon finding himself engulfed, he first used his 
trusty unicorn horn to mitigate the sickness, then zapped a wand of 
digging at the stomach lining of the beast.  With a wet splortch, he 
found himself next to Juiblex.  But there was also another, purple, 
demon there.

"Demogorgon attacks your saddled dragon!  Demogorgon attacks your 
saddled dragon!  Demogorgon stings your saddled dragon!"

Fortunately for Myrddin, he wasn't wearing any pants.  Because they 
would have been *quite* soiled.  He was hovering over water, so 
engraving and digging is out.  His cursed teleportation scrolls are 
stashed unhelpfully in his bag.  It's a special level, so even if he 
could teleport, he still couldn't, but big D could.

He swiped at the demon prince with Magicbane, and that damage, plus the 
damage inflicted when the silver dragon steed retaliated was enough to 
send the demon to the upstairs to heal.  With fortune briefly swinging 
in his favor, he quickly flew the rest of the say to solid ground, and 
engraved a protective rune with a wand of fire.  Next, he took another 
step, making sure Demogorgon was still preoccupied, and dug down with a 
wand and flew through the hole to the level below.

Magic mapping revealed it to be the level of Asmodeous.  Another 
no-teleport level.  That would be OK, though, for he would be protected 
on the upstairs, with another protective engraving, when he lured 
Demogorgon down.  He engraved, then climbed the stairs.

"Demogorgon attacks your saddled dragon!  Demogorgon attacks your 
saddled dragon!  Demogorgon stings your saddled dragon!"

Demogorgon apparently left his glasses at home that day.  Never stung 
Myrddin once.

Naturally, after that, the rest of the monsters didn't seem so scary. He 
visited Vlad's tower, and found a spellbook of magic missile on the top 
floor.  He searched through the remaining chests, and found another 
spellbook: Finger of Death.  He studied that one long and hard, only 
faintly distracted by some scuffling sounds.  When he finally finished, 
he turned the corner to deal with the master of the tower, only to find 
a vacant throne, with a candlestick just sitting there.  His pet 
Jabberwock must have gotten overly frisky.  He'd have to have a talk 
with it.

About the only supplies that he still needs now are a ring of slow 
digestion and a magic marker.  Perhaps in Orcus' town...

Justin "Myrddin" Hiltscher

